  "tools": [
   {
    "name": "flow",
    "description": "Live dashboard of the mattpocock-skills workflow (flow-board). When a mattpocock skill loads, its text ends with the step ids to report here. Silent bookkeeping: no need to mention it to the user; batch it with your other tool calls. Subagents, tests, commits, issues, PRs and workflow files are tracked automatically. Ticket status: a ticket that only waits for other tickets stays \"todo\" with \"blockedBy\" (shown as waiting); \"blocked\" is for a ticket that waits on something outside the lot (a decision or approval of the user, an access, someone else's work), with that reason in \"why\". Tasks you create in the tracker outside /to-tickets (review findings, anomalies, actions for the user) appear by themselves under \"found on the way\", outside the lot and its progress: send one in \"tickets\" (with its blockedBy) only when the lot depends on it. When the lot has groups and you add a ticket to it, send \"groups\" again with that ticket in the group of the work it belongs to. Each time you make a choice the user neither asked for nor approved (work on a ticket outside the queue, one approach among several, something skipped or deferred, a scope change, a fix beyond the ticket), report it at once in \"decided\": the user reviews these. Not for the routine steps a skill prescribes, nor for proposals you ask the user about. After each code review, report its findings in \"review\". The order and content of the reviews of a ticket and of a group are those of the steps the skill text lists (ticket: Standards and Spec axes, plus the bug pass /code-review in the same message for a sensitive area; group: /code-review high --fix, in a reviewer subagent that applies its fixes, then the security review). After the review of a finished group, judge whether a security review is worth it and send it in \"security\"; when it is, launch it yourself, in this discussion as in a linked session: a read-only reviewer subagent limited to the group's diff (never /security-review), then send its findings; the user decides on their fixes. A group the user delegated to a linked session from the dashboard stays in this workflow here: when that session ends, check its work, then send its tickets' statuses in \"set\" together with {\"returned\": [\"<group id>\"]}. Only when the user asks for it (a new workflow for some tickets, even while another one is active here), or to repair your own mistake: \"open\" starts a new workflow in this discussion without any skill, and the rest of the same call (tickets, groups...) goes to it; \"fix\" corrects the active workflow (title, current phase, step). Never open a workflow on your own initiative: when nothing is active here, the answer names the workflows to resume, so ask the user. To take tickets out of the wrong workflow, send \"remove\" in a call before the \"open\" one."
   }
